<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.OverviewView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:primitives="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Controls.Primitives;assembly=PresentationFramework"
             xmlns:gui="clr-namespace:Bws.Gui"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        WHAT THIS MACHINE LOOKS LIKE BEFORE ANYBODY ASKS IT ANYTHING - `G`, the first-run screen.

        THE SPECIFICATION'S OWN REASON, because it is better than anything this file could invent:
        an administrator opens the tool on an unknown server and sees several hundred rows, and that
        is the moment they either understand what it is for or close it. So the first thing on screen
        is a handful of numbers, each of them a question the machine has already been asked.

        IT REPLACES THE LIST RATHER THAN SITTING OVER IT - owner's decision, 2026-08-25, and it is
        the difference between this and EmptyState.xaml next door, which covers the middle of the
        grid and keeps the headings because the rows come back on the next key press.

        EVERY NUMBER IS A BUTTON AND CLICKING IT WRITES ITS QUERY INTO THE SEARCH BOX - `A5`'s
        promise on a screen with no chips. Which questions there are, and why each is worded the way
        it is, is decided in ViewModels/Overview.cs beside the reason for it.

        THREE CARDS RATHER THAN SIX LINES, FROM 2026-09-01, AND THE HISTORY IS THE ARGUMENT. The
        owner rejected this screen three times. The first rejection named a measured fault - a text
        style with no Foreground, so the numbers fell to system black at a contrast of 1.29 - and it
        was repaired at the style. The second and third were the same sentence after that repair, and
        the answer both times was another tuning of the same column: number brightened, qualification
        indented, a rule down its left edge.

        THEN IT WAS PHOTOGRAPHED, AND THE PICTURE SETTLES WHAT THE TUNINGS COULD NOT REACH. Six
        lines, three different left edges for the labels, short rules beside single digits reading as
        tick marks left over from a table, the numbers quieter than the sentences beside them, and
        the block in the top third of a window with four hundred empty pixels under it. None of that
        is a colour. The composition was the fault, so the composition is what changed.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT. Without them this is a tab stop that does nothing, in
        front of the numbers it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.

        NOT IsHitTestVisible="False", unlike the empty state, and the difference is the whole point:
        that panel must let clicks through to the rows underneath, and every number here is meant to
        be clicked.
    -->
    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!-- Not in a theme file, and that is `docs/10` trap 10: a dictionary read from source
             cannot name a type from this assembly. Compiled markup can. -->
        <gui:TilesAcross x:Key="CardsAcross" />

        <!--
            THE TWO STYLES THIS SCREEN'S BUTTONS WEAR ARE IN Themes/Overview.xaml since 2026-09-01,
            when the ratchet fired on this file three edits running. What could NOT go with them is
            below: a DataTemplate here wires Click to a method, and a handler named in markup needs
            the code-behind of the file declaring it - a resource dictionary has none.
        -->

        <!--
            ONE THING THAT HAS TO BE SAID ABOUT THE NUMBER OVER IT - a subtraction from it, or a
            widening of it. Drawn INSIDE the card of the number it belongs to, so belonging is
            carried by containment rather than by an indent and a hairline.

            IT IS STILL A BUTTON AND STILL CARRIES ITS OWN QUERY, which is the half of the honesty
            that matters: the headline leaves things out, and a person can click either exclusion and
            see exactly what was taken away. A number nobody can ask for is a number they have to
            take on trust - ViewModels/Overview.cs argues that in full.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="OverviewQualification">
            <Button Style="{StaticResource OverviewNumber}"
                    AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Spoken}"
                    Click="AskThis"
                    ToolTip="{Binding Query}">
                <Grid>
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="{StaticResource ColumnQualificationNumber}" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                               Text="{Binding CountText}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Right"
                               VerticalAlignment="Top"
                               Margin="{StaticResource MarginAfterNumber}"
                               Style="{StaticResource OverviewQualificationNumberText}" />

                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                               Text="{Binding Label}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               Style="{StaticResource OverviewQualificationText}" />
                </Grid>
            </Button>
        </DataTemplate>

        <!--
            ONE FINDING, AS A CARD. The number is the finding and is drawn as one - `G` puts a
            handful of numbers first so somebody who has never seen this tool learns what it is for
            without reading, and until today they were the quietest thing on the screen after the
            title.

            THE WHOLE CARD IS NOT ONE BUTTON, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. A card holds two or three
            different questions, so a click anywhere on it would have to guess which was meant. The
            headline is a button, each qualification is a button, the card is the surface they share.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="OverviewTile">
            <!--
                EVERY CARD AS TALL AS THE TALLEST, AND THE OTHER WAY WAS TRIED AND PHOTOGRAPHED ON
                2026-09-02. `VerticalAlignment="Top"` does remove the empty half of the first card
                and makes the screen worse: these cards carry a fill and a border, so three boxes
                ending at three heights read as a mistake rather than as three independent things.
                A row of equal cards is a row, a ragged one is a pile.
            -->
            <Border Background="{StaticResource SurfacePanel}"
                    BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfacePanelEdge}"
                    BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderTile}"
                    CornerRadius="{StaticResource CornerTile}"
                    Margin="{StaticResource MarginTile}"
                    Padding="{StaticResource PadTile}">
                <StackPanel>

                    <Button Style="{StaticResource OverviewNumber}"
                            DataContext="{Binding Headline}"
                            AutomationProperties.Name="{Binding Spoken}"
                            Click="AskThis"
                            ToolTip="{Binding Query}">
                        <StackPanel>
                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding CountText}">
                                <TextBlock.Style>
                                    <Style TargetType="TextBlock"
                                           BasedOn="{StaticResource OverviewNumberText}">
                                        <!--
                                            A ZERO IS THE ABSENCE OF A FINDING AND STOPS SHOUTING
                                            LIKE ONE - "0 orphans" is the line where nothing is.

                                            THE NUMBER GOES QUIET AND THE LABEL DOES NOT, which
                                            REVERSES half of a decision taken hours earlier the same
                                            day - and it can be reversed because that decision was
                                            made about a different drawing. On a flat line a subdued
                                            digit beside a full strength label read as a number
                                            greyed out by mistake, so the line went quiet together.
                                            At 32 inside a card, colour alone demotes it - and a card
                                            greyed end to end reads as DISABLED, which is the
                                            complaint this rebuild is answering.

                                            ASKED OF A BOOL RATHER THAN OF THE NUMBER, and the first
                                            version asked the number and did not fire.
                                            OverviewLine.Nothing carries that argument in full.
                                        -->
                                        <Style.Triggers>
                                            <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Nothing}" Value="True">
                                                <Setter Property="Foreground"
                                                        Value="{StaticResource TextSubdued}" />
                                            </DataTrigger>
                                        </Style.Triggers>
                                    </Style>
                                </TextBlock.Style>
                            </TextBlock>

                            <TextBlock Text="{Binding Label}"
                                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                                       Margin="{StaticResource MarginTileLabel}"
                                       Style="{StaticResource OverviewLabelText}" />
                        </StackPanel>
                    </Button>

                    <!--
                        NOTHING IS DRAWN WHEN THERE IS NOTHING TO SAY, which is complaint 12.2 of
                        `docs/11` - a heading over an empty space states an untruth. Collapsed rather
                        than left empty, because an empty element still spends its margin.
                    -->
                    <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Qualifications}"
                                  ItemTemplate="{StaticResource OverviewQualification}"
                                  Margin="{StaticResource MarginTileParts}">
                        <ItemsControl.Style>
                            <Style TargetType="ItemsControl">
                                <Style.Triggers>
                                    <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding HasQualifications}" Value="False">
                                        <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                                    </DataTrigger>
                                </Style.Triggers>
                            </Style>
                        </ItemsControl.Style>
                    </ItemsControl>
                </StackPanel>
            </Border>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <ScrollViewer x:Name="Scroll"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
        <!--
            THE BLOCK SITS IN THE MIDDLE OF THE WINDOW RATHER THAN AT THE TOP OF IT, from
            2026-09-01. This window is usually most of a screen and this screen is six numbers, so
            pinned to the top it left four hundred empty pixels underneath and read as unfinished
            rather than as sparse. Centred, the emptiness is above and below in equal measure, which
            is a composition rather than a remainder.

            THE MinHeight IS WHAT MAKES CENTRING POSSIBLE INSIDE A ScrollViewer, and it looks like
            decoration. A ScrollViewer measures content against infinite height, so VerticalAlignment
            there has nothing to centre within - the viewport's own height gives it something, and a
            content taller than the viewport simply scrolls as before.
        -->
        <Grid MinHeight="{Binding ViewportHeight, ElementName=Scroll}">
            <StackPanel MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverviewFindings}"
                        HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                        VerticalAlignment="Center"
                        Margin="{StaticResource PadPanel}">

                <!--
                    THE PROSE KEEPS THE PROSE WIDTH INSIDE THE WIDER BLOCK. Cards want about a
                    thousand points and a sentence wants about seventy characters, so the block is
                    sized for the cards and every sentence in it carries the older cap, WidthOverview.
                -->
                <!--
                    THE TITLE AND THE WAY OUT SHARE A LINE SINCE 2026-09-02 - backlog 269 answered
                    at its cause. The button stood under the cards, the cards stood in one row, so a
                    narrow window wrapped labels onto five lines and pushed the one control on this
                    screen below the edge. Nothing was lost, because the list scrolls - but the
                    first thing somebody sees on a machine they do not know was a screen with no
                    visible way off it. Nielsen's third heuristic.

                    WRAPPING WAS THE OBVIOUS FIX AND WAS WRONG THEN: two rows are TALLER than one.
                    The exit must not depend on the height of anything, and the top of a scroll is
                    the one place that holds at every size. Wrapping arrived after, as 277.

                    AND IT DOES NOT WEAR THE ACCENT, backlog 270, truer up here rather than less:
                    this is the way OFF the screen, not its action.
                -->
                <!--
                    THE GRID STRETCHES AND THE TITLE INSIDE IT DOES NOT, which is how the exit ends
                    up over the right edge of the last card rather than jammed against the heading.
                    The prose cap stays on the words, where the sentence about it above says it
                    belongs - a heading and a row of cards do not want the same measure.
                -->
                <Grid>
                    <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                        <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
                    </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                    <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                               Text="{DynamicResource gui.overview.title}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverview}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                               VerticalAlignment="Center"
                               Style="{StaticResource HeadingText}" />

                    <Button Grid.Column="1"
                            x:Name="DismissButton"
                            AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewDismiss"
                            Content="{DynamicResource gui.overview.dismiss}"
                            ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.overview.dismiss.hint}"
                            VerticalAlignment="Center"
                            Click="Dismiss" />
                </Grid>

                <!--
                    WHAT THE MACHINE READING IS DOING, ON THE SCREEN THAT HAS NO LIST TO SAY IT.
                    Backlog 263.

                    Until the chrome above this screen was collapsed, the count line beside the
                    search box carried "Reading the service control manager..." and the failure
                    message. That row is gone here, and the empty state that would otherwise carry it
                    is deliberately collapsed while this screen has the middle of the window - so
                    without this line a first run that could not read the manager would say nothing
                    at all, which is rule 8 in the first place anybody looks.

                    Red only for the failure. "Still reading" is ordinary news and passes in the
                    subdued voice the hint below uses - Says.ReadingFailed is what tells them apart,
                    because markup can only test for a value it is given.
                -->
                <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewReading"
                           Text="{Binding Says.ReadingMessage}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverview}"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
                    <TextBlock.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.ReadingMessage}" Value="">
                                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Says.ReadingFailed}" Value="True">
                                    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource MeaningRejected}" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </TextBlock.Style>
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Text="{DynamicResource gui.overview.hint}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverview}"
                           HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                           Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                           Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

                <!--
                    THE CARDS, IN ONE ROW OF EQUAL WIDTHS. A UniformGrid rather than a Grid with
                    three declared columns, because how many findings there are is data - the
                    questions live in ViewModels/Overview.cs and markup naming three of them would be
                    a second copy that drifts the day a fourth arrives.

                    THE AutomationId IS ON THE ItemsControl AND NOT ON A PANEL, which this window has
                    now paid for six times: WPF builds an automation peer for controls, not for
                    layout panels, so an identifier on a StackPanel sits on an element that is not in
                    the tree.
                -->
                <ItemsControl AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewNumbers"
                              AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource gui.overview.hint}"
                              Margin="{StaticResource MarginTileParts}"
                              ItemsSource="{Binding OverviewFindings}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource OverviewTile}">
                    <!--
                        THE ROW WRAPS SINCE 2026-09-02 - backlog 277, and it is the half of 269 that
                        the earlier repair UNBLOCKED rather than the one it replaced. `Rows="1"`
                        stood here, so a narrow window made LABELS five lines deep instead of making
                        cards narrower. Wrapping was the wrong answer while the way out stood under
                        the cards - two rows are taller than one - and stopped being wrong the
                        moment the exit moved up beside the title, where no height reaches it.

                        THE VIEWPORT AND NOT THE PANEL, which is the difference between working and
                        oscillating: this panel's width is decided BY the column count. TilesAcross
                        carries that argument and the arithmetic.
                    -->
                    <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                        <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                            <primitives:UniformGrid>
                                <primitives:UniformGrid.Columns>
                                    <MultiBinding Converter="{StaticResource CardsAcross}"
                                                  ConverterParameter="{StaticResource WidthOverviewCardLeast}">
                                        <Binding Path="ViewportWidth"
                                                 RelativeSource="{RelativeSource AncestorType=ScrollViewer}" />
                                        <Binding Path="Items.Count"
                                                 RelativeSource="{RelativeSource AncestorType=ItemsControl}" />
                                    </MultiBinding>
                                </primitives:UniformGrid.Columns>
                            </primitives:UniformGrid>
                        </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                </ItemsControl>

                <!--
                    THE NUMBER `G` PROMISES AND THIS BUILD CANNOT COUNT, said rather than left off.
                    It needs `D6`, the stock baseline, which the specification puts in Phase 3 - and
                    R3 in that same document asks for exactly this: mark the absence rather than
                    quietly show a bad result.

                    UNDER A RULE AND UNDER THE BUTTON SINCE 2026-09-01, and the SIZE is unchanged on
                    purpose. Demoting it by making it smaller would put it at eleven points, and
                    TextSizeSmall is twelve because eleven is below the floor TypeScaleGuards holds -
                    so the demotion is position and a hairline instead.
                -->
                <!--
                    THE RULE SPANS THE CARDS AND THE SENTENCE UNDER IT DOES NOT - the prose cap on
                    the right element. Capping the BORDER stopped the line halfway across a screen
                    the cards continue past, which reads as a drawing fault rather than a division.
                -->
                <Border BorderBrush="{StaticResource SurfaceRowLine}"
                        BorderThickness="{StaticResource BorderFooterRule}"
                        Margin="{StaticResource MarginTileParts}">
                    <TextBlock AutomationProperties.AutomationId="overviewNotYet"
                               Text="{Binding OverviewMissing}"
                               TextWrapping="Wrap"
                               MaxWidth="{StaticResource WidthOverview}"
                               HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                               Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}"
                               Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
                </Border>
            </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
    </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
